"""Original ILD chapter: fibrotic patterns, progression and exposure-related disease."""
from qbank_authoring import question,ref,finalize
PF=ref('ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT: IPF and Progressive Pulmonary Fibrosis Guideline, 2022','https://www.atsjournals.org/doi/pdf/10.1164/rccm.202202-0399ST')
HP=ref('ATS/JRS/ALAT: Diagnosis of Hypersensitivity Pneumonitis, 2020','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7397797/')
PA=ref('PANTHER-IPF: Prednisone, Azathioprine and N-Acetylcysteine Trial','https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa1113354')
NE=ref('FDA: Nerandomilast Prescribing Information, 2026','https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2026/218764s002lbl.pdf')
rows=[]
def q(*a,**k):rows.append(question(*a,**k))
q('FVC is reduced with a preserved FEV1/FVC ratio in suspected ILD. Which measurement confirms a restrictive ventilatory defect?', 'Reduced total lung capacity',[
 ('A low FVC alone in every case','Air trapping or incomplete effort can also lower FVC.'),('A high residual volume alone','That suggests gas trapping rather than establishing restriction.'),('A low peak expiratory flow alone','This is not a lung-volume definition.')],
 'Spirometry suggests but does not always prove restriction', '**TLC below the appropriate lower limit confirms the physiological defect.**',['Diffusion impairment and imaging add etiological information.','Extrapulmonary restriction can also lower TLC.'], '**Check quality and the full phenotype.**',['Do not label every low FVC as fibrotic ILD.','Use HRCT and clinical assessment to identify the cause.'],[['Spirometry','Suggestive low FVC/preserved ratio'],['Confirmation','Low TLC'],['Etiology','Needs further evidence']],[PF])
q('A smoker has relatively preserved lung volumes but severely reduced DLCO and exertional desaturation. CT shows upper emphysema and lower fibrosis. Which syndrome is suggestive?', 'Combined pulmonary fibrosis and emphysema',[
 ('Normal lung function because FVC is preserved','Opposing volume effects can mask substantial disease.'),('Pure asthma as the best fit for CT fibrosis and emphysema','The structural pattern is different.'),('A low DLCO proves anemia is the only cause','Anemia is a confounder but does not explain the CT findings.')],
 'Different structural effects can offset in a volume measurement', '**Emphysema increases compliance while fibrosis reduces it.**',['Gas transfer can be markedly abnormal despite near-normal volumes.','Pulmonary hypertension is an important concern.'], '**Interpret physiology with imaging and oxygen response.**',['Assess smoking cessation and disease-specific treatment.','Do not use FVC alone to estimate the entire burden.'],[['Volumes','Can appear preserved'],['DLCO','Often disproportionately low'],['CT','Emphysema plus fibrosis']],[PF],'clinical_scenario')
q('Which HRCT pattern is characteristic of UIP in an appropriate setting?', 'Basal and subpleural reticulation with honeycombing, with or without traction bronchiectasis',[
 ('Isolated diffuse centrilobular nodules with marked air trapping','This can suggest another process such as HP.'),('A single lobar consolidation as the defining chronic UIP pattern','That does not fit the usual fibrotic distribution.'),('A normal lung CT with only a pleural effusion','That does not establish UIP.')],
 'A pattern name describes morphology and distribution', '**Honeycombing is a key feature of definite UIP.**',['Probable and indeterminate categories have different imaging criteria.','The absence of honeycombing does not exclude all fibrotic disease.'], '**Use expert radiology and multidisciplinary interpretation.**',['Search for features that suggest an alternative diagnosis.','The pattern does not by itself establish an idiopathic cause.'],[['Distribution','Basal/subpleural'],['Fibrosis','Reticulation/traction'],['Definite UIP feature','Honeycombing']],[PF])
q('A UIP pattern is present on CT. What is required before labeling the disease idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis?', 'Exclude relevant exposures, connective tissue disease, drugs and other identifiable causes',[
 ('UIP is synonymous with IPF in every patient','UIP can occur in RA, exposure-related disease and other conditions.'),('A negative ANA alone excludes all autoimmune ILD','Clinical and broader context-directed assessment are necessary.'),('A biopsy can replace every exposure history','Histology must be interpreted with context.')],
 'Idiopathic is an etiological conclusion', '**IPF requires an appropriate clinical setting and exclusion of known causes.**',['Occupational, environmental and medication history can change treatment.','Autoimmune features may be subtle.'], '**Use a multidisciplinary diagnosis.**',['Do not prescribe a uniform immunosuppressive or antifibrotic strategy from a pattern alone.','Reassess when new systemic features emerge.'],[['UIP','Morphological pattern'],['IPF','Idiopathic disease diagnosis'],['Process','Exclude causes and integrate data']],[PF])
q('An older patient has a convincing clinical IPF context and definite UIP HRCT after alternatives are excluded. Is surgical lung biopsy always required?', 'No; an appropriate high-confidence multidisciplinary diagnosis can be made without surgical biopsy',[
 ('Every UIP CT requires an operation regardless of risk','The guideline does not mandate that.'),('No uncertain ILD ever benefits from tissue','Selected cases can need biopsy.'),('A biopsy is risk free and should be done only for completeness','Procedural risk and diagnostic utility matter.')],
 'Tissue is obtained to resolve uncertainty that changes care', '**A characteristic pattern can provide sufficient confidence in the right setting.**',['Age, physiology and comorbidity affect biopsy safety.','Cryobiopsy is a selected alternative in experienced centers for appropriate cases.'], '**Make the procedural decision collaboratively.**',['Do not expose a patient to risk when the result is unlikely to change management.','Document residual uncertainty and follow-up.'],[['High confidence','May avoid surgery'],['Uncertainty','Consider useful tissue'],['Decision','Utility, expertise and risk']],[PF],'clinical_scenario')
q('What is the main therapeutic expectation from antifibrotic treatment in established IPF?', 'Slowing functional decline rather than reliably reversing established fibrosis',[
 ('A guaranteed complete anatomical cure','Current treatment does not promise that.'),('Immediate rescue of every acute hypoxemic episode','Acute deterioration requires separate assessment.'),('No monitoring is required once treatment begins','Adverse effects and disease progression need follow-up.')],
 'Disease modification and structural reversal are different outcomes', '**Pirfenidone and nintedanib have established evidence for slowing decline.**',['Newer options must be considered under current authorization and evidence.','Liver, GI and other adverse effects affect choice.'], '**Provide comprehensive ILD care.**',['Rehabilitation, oxygen assessment and transplant referral can be important.','Do not wait for medication failure to discuss supportive needs.'],[['Goal','Reduce pace of decline'],['Not promised','Complete reversal'],['Care','Medication plus comprehensive support']],[PF,NE])
q('Why is routine prednisone-azathioprine-NAC triple therapy avoided in IPF?', 'PANTHER-IPF found increased harm with that combination',[
 ('It is the standard proven mortality-reducing regimen for IPF','The trial showed an unfavorable safety outcome.'),('The finding means all immunosuppression is forbidden in every ILD','Other inflammatory or autoimmune ILDs have different treatment evidence.'),('NAC is a direct replacement for all modern antifibrotics','That is unsupported.')],
 'An ILD diagnosis must precede transfer of treatment evidence', '**The triple-therapy arm was stopped because of excess adverse outcomes.**',['The result applies to IPF rather than every diffuse lung disease.','Historical practice changed because of randomized evidence.'], '**Avoid a generic “fibrosis equals steroids” rule.**',['Identify immune-mediated and exposure-related alternatives.','Use disease-specific current guidance.'],[['Disease','IPF'],['Studied combination','Prednisone/azathioprine/NAC'],['Result','Harm rather than routine benefit']],[PA,PF])
q('Which newer fibrosis medicine is a preferential PDE4B inhibitor with adult IPF and PPF indications in the current U.S. label?', 'Nerandomilast',[
 ('Omalizumab','It is an anti-IgE biologic for selected allergic disease.'),('Mepolizumab','It targets IL-5.'),('Roflumilast as an interchangeable licensed IPF drug','The COPD PDE4 drug does not have the same indication or evidence.')],
 'A pathway family does not make medicines interchangeable', '**The 2026 label includes both IPF and progressive pulmonary fibrosis.**',['The drug slows functional decline in the studied populations rather than curing scar.','Dose, interactions and adverse effects follow the current product guidance.'], '**Assess local access and authorization.**',['Do not describe a late-2025 approval as still purely experimental.','Choose combination or alternative therapy with an ILD specialist.'],[['Drug','Nerandomilast'],['Target','Preferential PDE4B'],['Indications','Adult IPF and PPF in current U.S. label']],[NE])
q('What defines PPF in a non-IPF fibrotic ILD under the 2022 ATS/ERS framework?', 'At least two of worsening symptoms, physiological progression and radiological progression within one year without an alternative explanation',[
 ('Any single low FVC at diagnosis','A low baseline value is not the same as progression.'),('A positive autoimmune antibody alone','This does not define progressive behavior.'),('Every non-IPF ILD is automatically progressive','The course varies.')],
 'PPF describes behavior rather than a separate underlying etiology', '**The definition requires a fibrotic ILD other than IPF and documented change.**',['Physiological criteria include specified absolute FVC or DLCO declines.','Infection, pulmonary vascular disease and other explanations must be considered.'], '**Treat the underlying disease as well as progression when appropriate.**',['Antifibrotic eligibility depends on the full context and current options.','Do not erase the original diagnosis by assigning PPF.'],[['Domains','Symptoms, physiology and imaging'],['Requirement','At least two within a year'],['Exclusions','Alternative explanation']],[PF])
q('FVC falls from 72% to 66% predicted over ten months in a non-IPF fibrotic ILD, with no alternative cause. How does this relate to the PPF physiological criterion?', 'The six-percentage-point absolute decline meets the FVC progression component',[
 ('It is only a 0.6% absolute decline','The absolute difference is six percentage points.'),('It cannot count unless FVC falls by at least 20 points','The framework uses at least five absolute percentage points.'),('This single component automatically satisfies the full two-domain definition','Another qualifying domain is needed.')],
 'Distinguish absolute percentage-point change from relative change', '**The FVC criterion is an absolute decline of at least 5% predicted within one year.**',['A DLCO decline of at least 10% predicted is another physiological route.','The full PPF definition still requires the stated combination of domains.'], '**Verify measurement quality and cause.**',['Interpret serial tests consistently.','Use the result with symptoms and imaging.'],[['FVC change','72 to 66'],['Absolute decline','Six points'],['Meaning','One physiological progression component']],[PF],'clinical_scenario')
q('Fibrotic ILD is accompanied by a history of bird exposure, mosaic attenuation and expiratory air trapping. What diagnosis should be actively evaluated?', 'Hypersensitivity pneumonitis',[
 ('IPF is proven because any fibrosis is present','Exposure and small-airway features suggest an alternative.'),('An acute bacterial lobar pneumonia as the only process','The chronic diffuse pattern and exposure point elsewhere.'),('A positive exposure history alone establishes HP','Exposure is important but must be integrated with imaging and other evidence.')],
 'Exposure and airway-centered clues can redirect the diagnosis', '**HP can be nonfibrotic or fibrotic.**',['A three-density pattern and air trapping can be helpful imaging features.','The exposure can be occult or unrecognized initially.'], '**Take a detailed home and occupational history.**',['Use multidisciplinary integration with BAL or tissue when needed.','Do not assume all sensitized people have disease.'],[['Exposure','Bird/mold or other antigen'],['Imaging clue','Air trapping/mosaic pattern'],['Diagnosis','Integrated evidence']],[HP],'clinical_scenario')
q('How should BAL lymphocytosis be interpreted in suspected HP?', 'As supportive evidence within the exposure-imaging-pathology framework, not a standalone diagnosis',[
 ('It proves HP regardless of all other data','Other diseases can produce lymphocytic BAL.'),('Its absence excludes every fibrotic HP case','Fibrotic disease can have less striking lymphocytosis.'),('It replaces all exposure investigation','The antigen question remains central.')],
 'A sample adds probability rather than an infallible label', '**The 2020 guideline integrates three major diagnostic domains.**',['Lymphocyte proportion depends on phenotype and other factors.','Smoking and prior therapy can affect interpretation.'], '**Use the result in multidisciplinary discussion.**',['Assess whether further tissue would change care.','Avoid unnecessary invasive testing after high confidence is reached.'],[['BAL','Supportive cellular information'],['Other domains','Exposure and imaging'],['Decision','Integrated confidence']],[HP])
q('What is a central management principle when a credible HP antigen exposure is identified?', 'Reduce or eliminate exposure while assessing disease activity, fibrosis and the need for additional specialist treatment',[
 ('Medication makes ongoing exposure irrelevant','Continued antigen exposure can sustain injury.'),('Exposure removal guarantees complete reversal of established fibrosis','Recovery varies and scar may persist.'),('Every suspected exposure requires the same immunosuppressive regimen','Treatment depends on phenotype and progression.')],
 'Remove the driver when feasible', '**Environmental remediation and avoidance can be clinically important.**',['The practical source may require detailed investigation.','Fibrotic progression can continue despite removal.'], '**Follow symptoms, lung function and imaging as appropriate.**',['Use an individualized inflammatory and antifibrotic strategy.','Balance remediation burden with the strength of exposure evidence.'],[['Driver','Inhaled antigen'],['First principle','Exposure intervention'],['Follow-up','Activity and progression']],[HP,PF])
q('A patient with established fibrotic ILD develops acute hypoxemia and new bilateral ground-glass change. What is the immediate diagnostic principle?', 'Evaluate infection, edema, embolism and other triggers while considering an acute ILD exacerbation',[
 ('Every new opacity is automatically sterile inflammation requiring only steroids','Treatable alternate or coexisting causes must be assessed.'),('Chronic fibrosis prevents pulmonary edema','Cardiac and volume causes can still occur.'),('Antifibrotics alone provide immediate rescue for all acute deterioration','Acute supportive and cause-directed care are needed.')],
 'Acute change on chronic fibrosis has a broad differential', '**An exacerbation diagnosis requires the appropriate time course and imaging context.**',['Infection and aspiration can be relevant triggers.','The evidence for some acute immunosuppressive strategies is limited and disease specific.'], '**Stabilize gas exchange and obtain urgent specialist care.**',['Review support goals and transplant status when appropriate.','Do not delay treatment of an identified reversible cause.'],[['Acute findings','New hypoxemia/opacities'],['Differential','Infection, edema, PE and exacerbation'],['Priority','Support plus targeted evaluation']],[PF],'clinical_scenario')
